      "designInstructions": "# Corporate Swiss Precision Design Language\n\nThis design system is built on the International Typographic Style, emphasizing absolute clarity, institutional trust, and objective precision.\n\n### Layout Signatures\n- **12-Column Swiss Grid**: Use a strict 12-column grid for all page layouts. Headlines should often span 7-8 columns to command authority.\n- **Hairline Rules**: Use 1px rules in (#2A3439) at 0.1 opacity for section dividers and 1.0 opacity for primary component boundaries.\n- **Asymmetric Balance**: Balance large typography blocks with clinical macro-photography or high-density data tables.\n\n### Border & Shape Eccentricities\n- **Sharp Geometry**: 0px corner radius for all primary elements.\n- **Boxed Compositions**: Use minimal black borders around key imagery or data blocks to give them a \"physically printed\" appearance.\n\n### Decorative Motifs\n- **Scanning HUDs**: Use minimalist overlays on imagery featuring active scan lines in (--k-color-accent) and pulsed status indicators.\n- **Index Numbers**: Number key sections or steps using small, monospaced digits (e.g., 01, 02) to reinforce a rigorous process.\n\n### Spacing & Density Rhythm\n- **Bold Margins**: Generous use of white space to ensure maximum legibility and a premium, uncluttered feel.\n- **Capability Ledgers**: Present features as editorial spec-sheets with clear tabular alignment instead of cards.\n\n### Micro-Details\n- **Typography**: Plus Jakarta Sans (Extrabold) for headlines with tight tracking (-0.03em). IBM Plex Sans for body copy to provide a technical, functional contrast.\n- **Grayscale Accents**: Use grayscale imagery with high contrast and subtle navy tints to maintain a professional \"CEO-approved\" vibe.",
      "created": "2026-06-13T08:32:20.128668+00:00",
      "modified": "2026-06-13T08:32:20.128668+00:00"
    }
